// batch aj: berlim, huaihai, inchon, dienbienphu, baiadosporcos
Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "katyusha": "A Soviet rocket launcher, mounted on a truck. It fires dozens of rockets in a few seconds and became a symbol of Red Army artillery.",
  "volkssturm": "The 'people's storm': a militia created by Nazi Germany at the end of the war, made up of old men and boys who had not yet been drafted, with almost no training and improvised weapons.",
  "frente": "In the Red Army, a group of armies that covered one sector of the battle line, under a single commander. At Berlin three fronts attacked: Zhukov's, Konev's and Rokossovsky's.",
  "exército de tanques": "A large Soviet formation built around tanks and motorized infantry. It was launched through the hole the artillery opened, to run behind the enemy line and encircle.",
  "grupo de exércitos": "Several armies brought together under a single command, covering a large sector of the front.",
  "desembarque anfíbio": "An attack in which troops leave ships directly onto an enemy beach. It is one of the hardest operations in war: the soldiers arrive exposed, and everything depends on timing, tide and coordination.",
  "napalm": "Gasoline in gel form, which sticks to what it hits and burns. Dropped from aircraft, it was widely used in the Korean War.",
  "cabeça de praia": "The strip of land that the troops of a landing take and hold, so that the rest of the army can arrive behind them.",
  "paraquedistas": "Soldiers dropped from aircraft by parachute. They arrive behind or inside the enemy line, but without heavy artillery.",
  "ponto de apoio": "A fortified position, with trenches, barbed wire and shelters of concrete or earth, able to hold out on its own even when surrounded. Dien Bien Phu was a set of strongpoints with women's names.",
  "ponte aérea": "Supply carried only by aircraft, when the roads are cut. It works as long as the airstrip and the planes hold out.",
  "viet minh": "A front created in 1941 by communists and nationalists to fight for Vietnam's independence. In 1954, its army, commanded by Giáp, was fighting France.",
  "legião estrangeira": "A corps of the French army made up of volunteers from many countries, under French officers. It fought at Dien Bien Phu.",
  "tirailleurs": "Soldiers recruited in the French colonies, such as Algeria and Morocco, under French officers.",
  "sapa": "A trench dug in a zigzag until very close to the enemy position, to reach the attack protected from fire. It was the Vietnamese method at Dien Bien Phu.",
  "fogo direto": "Cannon fire aimed in a straight line at a target that can be seen. The Vietnamese did this from inside tunnels in the hills, which the French did not expect.",
  "contrabateria": "Artillery fire against enemy artillery, to silence its guns. At Dien Bien Phu the French were unable to do this.",
  "kuomintang": "The Chinese Nationalist Party, led by Chiang Kai-shek, which governed China and fought the communists in the civil war.",
  "exército de libertação popular": "The army of the Chinese Communist Party. In the Huaihai Campaign, its two great groups were the East China Field Army and the Central Plains Field Army.",
  "campanha": "A set of battles linked by a single objective, in one region and one period. Huaihai was a 66-day campaign, not a one-day battle.",
  "brigada 2506": "The force of Cuban exiles trained by the CIA to invade Cuba, with about 1,400 men.",
  "cia": "The United States espionage agency. It planned, trained and financed the Bay of Pigs invasion.",
  "guerra fria": "The political, economic and military contest between the United States and the Soviet Union after 1945, without direct war between the two.",
  "embargo": "A ban on trade with a country, used as political pressure.",
  "guerrilha": "War by small, mobile groups, who attack by surprise and vanish into the bush or among the population."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  berlim: {
    war: "Second World War, European front: the Soviet offensive on Berlin (16 April to 2 May 1945)",
    hook: "The city Hitler ordered defended to the end became, in two weeks, the field of the last great battle of the war in Europe.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In January 1945, the Red Army launched the Vistula–Oder offensive and advanced hundreds of kilometers across Poland, until it stopped at the Oder River, a few dozen kilometers east of Berlin. There it stayed for about three months, gathering ammunition, tanks and men. On the other side, Germany no longer had any way to win the war: its last great counterattack, in Hungary, had failed in mid-March.",
        "The Western Allies, who were on the other side of Germany, had no plan to take Berlin. The city was left to the Soviets, and Stalin was in a hurry to get there, according to many historians before the Americans and the British changed their minds.",
        { h: "The road to Berlin" },
        { timeline: [
          ["12 Jan 1945", "The Soviet Vistula–Oder offensive begins, and stops at the Oder River."],
          ["6 Mar", "Helmuth Reymann takes over the defense of the city of Berlin."],
          ["20 Mar", "Gotthard Heinrici takes over Army Group Vistula, in place of Heinrich Himmler."],
          ["1 Apr", "Stalin summons Zhukov and Konev to Moscow and settles the plan and the date of the attack."],
          ["13 Apr", "Vienna falls to the Soviets."],
          ["16 Apr", "The Berlin Operation begins, with the attack on the Seelow Heights."]
        ]},
        "Heinrici was a specialist in defense. Instead of taking the first blow at the river's edge, where Soviet artillery would fall with full force, he planned to defend the Seelow Heights, behind the Oder. The river valley was flooded, turning into a swamp, and three lines of defense were dug, the last of them some 16 to 24 km behind the front, toward Berlin.",
        "On 9 March the defense plan for the city itself was fixed, called Clausewitz. It was an enormous city to defend with scarce troops: what remained were worn-out divisions of the army and the Waffen-SS, and improvised militias."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Georgy Zhukov, 48 years old" },
        "Commander of the 1st Belorussian Front, the one that would attack Berlin from the east, head-on. He had been Stalin's man in the Red Army's greatest victories, from Moscow to Stalingrad. After the war, he wrote that Stalin had promised him the honor of taking Berlin. Konev remembered nothing of the kind.",
        { h: "Ivan Konev, 47 years old" },
        "Commander of the 1st Ukrainian Front, south of Zhukov. He knew that the dividing line between the two fronts, drawn by the General Staff, ended near a little town called Lübben. Konev said afterward that he had insisted on this, and Zhukov disputed it. What is certain is that the map was erased from Lübben onward, leaving the race open. It is hard to know who said what, because both marshals wrote their memoirs after the war and had an interest in coming out well in history.",
        { h: "Gotthard Heinrici and Helmuth Weidling" },
        "Heinrici commanded Army Group Vistula, the defense on the Oder, and was relieved of command at the end of April. Weidling took command of the defense of the city of Berlin around 23 April (sources give the 23rd, 24th or 26th). He inherited a small garrison, made up of the remnants of divisions, the Hitler Youth and old men of the Volkssturm.",
        { h: "Adolf Hitler" },
        "He was in the bunker, under the Reich Chancellery, giving orders to units that often no longer existed. He killed himself on the afternoon of 30 April, together with Eva Braun."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Soviets gathered, in three [[fronts|frente]], about 2.5 million men (including some 78,500 Polish soldiers), 6,250 tanks, 41,600 guns and mortars, 3,255 [[katyusha|katyusha]] launchers and 7,500 aircraft. Zhukov's front alone, which would attack Seelow, had close to a million men.",
        "The Germans had much less. At Seelow, Theodor Busse's Ninth Army had about 110,000 men, 14 divisions and 587 tanks. Inside Berlin, Weidling had some 45,000 soldiers and about 40,000 of the [[Volkssturm|volkssturm]]. There were also policemen and young people of the Hitler Youth. The exact numbers vary widely from one historian to another, and the Germans had more troops scattered to the north and south.",
        { note: "The counts vary: what is called 'German forces in Berlin' sometimes includes only the city garrison, sometimes the whole army on the three fronts of the operation. Here, we use each number for what it really measures." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "From the Oder to Berlin, the terrain was flat farmland, cut by canals, rivers, lakes and forests. The Seelow Heights rise some 48 meters above the Oder valley. That seems little, but, after the Germans flooded the floodplain, Soviet tanks could only climb by a few paths, and German artillery dominated the slope.",
        { h: "The battle in ten movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the attack on Seelow. In the early hours of 16 April, some 9,000 guns and katyushas fired more than 500,000 shells in 30 minutes, and more than a hundred searchlights were switched on to blind the defenders. Almost none of it worked: the Germans had pulled back to the second line, and the dust and smoke blinded the searchlights themselves. By the end of the day, Zhukov's front had advanced 4 to 6 km, far less than the plan foresaw, and the second line of defense still held.",
        "Second, Zhukov threw into the fight earlier than planned his [[tank armies|exército de tanques]], which were supposed to wait for the infantry to open the way. Stalin warned Zhukov that Konev would send his tanks north, toward Berlin, if Zhukov's front did not move. On the 17th, Konev, who had crossed the Neisse, attacked the southern sector and weakened Heinrici's position.",
        "Third, on 19 April the last line of defense broke, and the Ninth Army was enveloped. The road to Berlin was open. The count of dead at Seelow is debated: from fewer than 10,000 to more than 30,000 Soviets, depending on the historian, and some 12,000 Germans. The Soviets lost at least 727 tanks there.",
        "Fourth, on 20 April, Hitler's birthday, Zhukov's artillery began bombarding the center of the city, and did not stop until the surrender. At the same time, Konev's tanks advanced from the south, toward the southern districts of Berlin.",
        "Fifth, the encirclement. By 24 and 25 April, the two tank armies met west of the city, near Ketzin, and Berlin was isolated. On the 25th, farther south, Soviet and American soldiers met at Torgau, on the Elbe River, cutting Germany in two.",
        { h: "The Halbe pocket" },
        "Sixth and seventh movements. The Ninth Army, of some 80,000 men (counting civilians), was surrounded in the Spree forest, southeast of Berlin. Between 24 April and 1 May, it tried to break out to the west, toward Walther Wenck's Twelfth Army. There were three attempts, the third, on the night of the 28th, opened a corridor near Halbe. The count of who escaped varies: from some 25,000 to some 30,000. About 24,000 Germans are buried in the Halbe cemetery.",
        { h: "Inside the city" },
        "Eighth, the street-by-street fighting. On 26 April the Soviets attacked Tempelhof airport, and, at the start of the 29th, the 3rd Shock Army crossed the Moltke Bridge, heading for the Reichstag. The fighting went district by district.",
        "Ninth, the Reichstag. On the morning of 30 April, artillery supported the attack on the parliament building, which the Soviets entered on the night of the same day. The building only came fully under Soviet control on 2 May. On the afternoon of the 30th, Hitler and Eva Braun killed themselves in the bunker.",
        "Tenth, the surrender. In the early hours of 1 May, General Hans Krebs sought out the Soviet general Chuikov to talk about surrender. Goebbels and his wife killed themselves that afternoon. On the night of 1 to 2 May, part of the defenders tried to break the encirclement. On the morning of the 2nd, the Soviets took the Reich Chancellery, and Weidling surrendered, at 6 o'clock."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The Berlin garrison surrendered on 2 May, but fighting continued to the west, northwest and southwest until 8 May, when Germany signed the general surrender (9 May, by Moscow time). Between 2 and 3 May, two German generals surrendered to the Americans, and Wenck crossed the Elbe to surrender to the Americans on 7 May.",
        "Soviet losses, according to the Russian historian Krivosheev, were 361,367 men, of whom 81,116 dead or missing. Another historian, John Erickson, gives 304,877. On the German side, 92,000 to 100,000 dead are estimated, and the Soviets said they had captured close to 480,000 prisoners. About 125,000 civilians died.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the last great battle of the war in Europe and the end of the Third Reich. Berlin was left in ruins, with more than a million people homeless, and in June 1945, each resident received, on average, 64% of a daily ration of 1,240 calories. The city was divided into sectors among the victors. With the Cold War, it became two cities, separated, from 1961 to 1989, by a wall.",
        "On the Soviet side, 402 servicemen received the title of Hero of the Soviet Union for Berlin, and about 1.1 million won the medal 'For the Capture of Berlin'.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The race: the idea that Stalin pitted Zhukov and Konev against each other comes from the marshals' own memoirs and from historians such as Antony Beevor. It is plausible, but the details (who asked for the line at Lübben, who promised Berlin to whom) are disputed." },
        { note: "The cost of Seelow: there were critics who said Zhukov should have gone around the heights. The historian Isaev says that attacking Seelow was the quickest way to meet Konev and cut off the Ninth Army." },
        { note: "The searchlights: several sources speak of 143, and a Seelow memorial speaks of 120. They were supposed to blind the Germans, but the dust blinded the searchlights themselves and, according to the memorial, the light ended up showing the attackers." },
        { note: "The civilians: many German women suffered sexual violence from Soviet soldiers during the capture of the city. It is a well-documented fact, but the number of victims is much debated, and so we give no number here." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  huaihai: {
    war: "Chinese Civil War (1945–1949): the Huaihai Campaign, from 6 November 1948 to 10 January 1949",
    hook: "In 66 days, the communists swallowed, one piece at a time, the largest Nationalist army left in China.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "After Japan surrendered, in 1945, China became again the stage of a war between two sides that had already been fighting for almost twenty years: the [[Kuomintang|kuomintang]], of Chiang Kai-shek, which governed the country, and the Communist Party of Mao Zedong. By 1948, the communists were taking Manchuria and preparing the march south.",
        "The name of the campaign joins two place words: the Huai River and Haizhou, on the coast. The plan came from one of the communist commanders, Su Yu. On 24 September 1948, the communists took Jinan, in Shandong province, and Su Yu proposed advancing south, toward the region between the Huai River and the sea. Mao approved and added an objective: to destroy the Nationalist 7th Army, at Xin'an.",
        { h: "The road to Huaihai" },
        { timeline: [
          ["24 Sep 1948", "The communists take Jinan."],
          ["22 and 24 Oct", "Zhengzhou and Kaifeng fall, to the Central Plains Field Army."],
          ["1 Nov", "Chen Yi and Deng Xiaoping are given command of the operations."],
          ["5 Nov", "Chiang Kai-shek decides to make the defense from Xuzhou."],
          ["6 Nov", "The campaign begins."]
        ]},
        "Xuzhou was the meeting point of two railways, one east to west (Longhai) and another north to south, between Tianjin and Pukou, near Nanjing. Whoever controlled it cut the link between north and south, and the Nationalists gathered many troops there."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "On the communist side" },
        "Mao Zedong approved the plan and followed from afar. Those who commanded the operation in the field were the pair Chen Yi and Deng Xiaoping, who received command on 1 November, and who for the first time brought together two great field armies (the East China one, of Su Yu and Chen Yi, and the Central Plains one, of Liu Bocheng and Deng Xiaoping). It was the communists' first joint operation of such size. The declared objective was not to take territory, but to destroy enemy armies.",
        { h: "On the Nationalist side" },
        "Chiang Kai-shek decided the broad lines, and even sent a handwritten order to Du by air, on 3 December. At Xuzhou, Liu Zhi was the commander of the defense headquarters, and Du Yuming the field commander. Du wanted to attack the Central Plains Army, but Chiang and Liu Zhi thought the plan too risky and ordered a direct relief of the 7th Army. The two commanders whose armies ended up surrounded were Huang Baitao (7th Army) and Huang Wei (12th Army).",
        { h: "Those who switched sides" },
        "At several moments, Nationalist officers who were in fact working for the communists were decisive. He Jifeng and Zhang Kexia, infiltrated Nationalist generals, took their army corps to the other side. Later, General Liao Yunzhou, who was secretly a member of the Communist Party, did the same with his 110th Division."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The numbers are the most debated of the whole campaign. The Nationalists had about 800,000 men. The communists appear with very different numbers: about 600,000 in many counts, and as many as 1,060,000 if irregulars are added (660,000 regulars and 400,000 irregulars), a number that Wikipedia takes from the same 2015 thesis it uses for the Nationalists.",
        { note: "The troop and casualty numbers for this campaign come in large part from official Chinese sources and are used with caution by historians." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "A vast plain, north of Nanjing, cut by rivers, canals and railways, in the provinces of Shandong, Jiangsu, Anhui and Henan. The peasants' villages were the communists' base of support.",
        { h: "The campaign in ten movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the positions. On 5 November, Chiang decides to center the defense on Xuzhou. The communists were already around it: the East China Field Army to the east, and the Central Plains one to the west and south.",
        "Second, the betrayal of 8 November. On the night of the 8th, Nationalist commanders who were communist infiltrators (He Jifeng and Zhang Kexia) took their army corps to the other side, about 23,000 men, according to one source. This cut the Longhai railway, and the 7th Army had nowhere left to flee.",
        "Third, Nianzhuang. Huang Baitao's 7th Army was surrounded in the village of Nianzhuang, some 30 km east of Xuzhou. In a few days, the 63rd Corps was destroyed, and the army split in two.",
        "Fourth, the relief that never arrived. From 13 to 22 November, the relief columns sent from Xuzhou were stopped. To the south, Suxian fell between 13 and 15 November, isolating Xuzhou even more.",
        "Fifth, on 22 November, Huang Baitao killed himself, and the 7th Army was destroyed. The counts vary: about 25,000 dead and some 80,000 wounded or prisoners, according to one source; another speaks of about 100,000 men destroyed in 15 days. Only 2,000 to 3,000 escaped.",
        { h: "The 12th Army at Shuangduiji" },
        "Sixth, the trap in the south. Huang Wei's 12th Army, of about 120,000 men, marched north, with the aim of reopening the railway between Xuzhou and Bengbu. On 24 November, it crossed the Kuai River and fell into a trap. On the 25th, it was surrounded in an area of 7.5 km around the village of Shuangduiji, by more than 280,000 communists. In the attempt to break out, the 110th Division, commanded by Liao Yunzhou, switched sides, and the other divisions that followed it were ambushed and pushed back.",
        "Seventh, on 28 November, Chiang ordered Xuzhou evacuated, and the departure began on the night of 30 November. Du Yuming left with his armies to the southwest. It was a winter retreat, with the communists all around.",
        "Eighth, Chenguanzhuang. At the start of December, Du Yuming's armies were stopped and surrounded at Chenguanzhuang. An escape attempt, on 6 December, was repelled, and the 16th Army lost about 30,000 men, dead or captured. The bad winter weather hampered the delivery of supplies by air.",
        "Ninth, the end of the 12th Army. On 6 December the communists launched the final attack, from three sides, with the technique of 'trenches against trenches'. Liu Bocheng and Chen Yi sent an ultimatum, which Huang Wei refused. On 15 December, at nightfall, the 12th Army was destroyed, and Huang Wei captured, after his tank broke down in a last attempt to flee. According to Wikipedia, more than 46,000 Nationalists died and more than 50,000 were captured, and the communists came away with 870 guns and 15 tanks.",
        "Tenth, the end of Du Yuming. The final attack on Chenguanzhuang began on 6 January 1949 and ended on 10 January. Du Yuming was captured. Li Mi, another commander, escaped."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The Nationalists lost about 555,000 men, of whom some 327,000 were captured or switched sides. Communist losses were around 134,000. Both counts have source problems and should be read as estimates.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the second of the three great campaigns that decided the civil war (Liaoshen, in Manchuria, Huaihai and Pingjin, in Beijing and Tianjin), and the one that caused the heaviest losses to the Kuomintang. Chiang announced that he would step aside and left office on 21 January 1949. American military aid dwindled. The People's Liberation Army moved on to the Yangtze River, and on 1 October 1949 the People's Republic of China was proclaimed.",
        "On the winning side, the campaign became a myth of popular mobilization. According to a phrase attributed to the commander Chen Yi, the victory was 'pushed in wheelbarrows' by the peasants. Chinese state media speak of 5.43 million peasants mobilized, about six for every soldier.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The people's numbers: the 5.43 million peasants comes from a single source, Chinese state media, and not from an independent study. The importance of the porters is consensus, the exact number is not." },
        { note: "Names and spellings: Chinese names appear in different transcription systems, and Du Yuming sometimes appears as 'Du Yiming'." },
        { note: "The death of Qiu Qingquan, Nationalist commander of the 2nd Army: the Wikipedia text speaks of 'killed', and the box on the same page says suicide. The sources read do not agree." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  inchon: {
    war: "Korean War (1950–1953): the landing at Incheon, 15 September 1950",
    hook: "A general bet everything on the worst possible port, precisely because no one believed anyone would be crazy enough to attack there.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "On 25 June 1950, North Korea invaded South Korea. Within a few weeks, the South Korean forces and the American ones that arrived to help were pushed back into the southeastern corner of the peninsula, in what was called the Pusan Perimeter. In August, the North Koreans attacked the perimeter and failed. The last great offensive, the Great Naktong Offensive, began on 1 September and also failed, but left both sides exhausted.",
        "General Douglas MacArthur, commander of the UN forces, wanted an amphibious landing to retake the Seoul region. But the earlier plans, Bluehearts (July) and Chromite (23 July), were abandoned because the troops that were to be used were sent to the perimeter. MacArthur then gathered what was left: the Army's 7th Division, the last reserve of the United States in the Far East, and the Marines.",
        { h: "The road to Incheon" },
        { timeline: [
          ["25 Jun 1950", "North Korea invades the South."],
          ["23 Aug", "MacArthur presents in Tokyo the plan for a landing at Incheon."],
          ["28 Aug", "The American Joint Chiefs of Staff approve the plan."],
          ["1 Sep", "Lieutenant Eugene Clark lands on an island near Incheon to spy."],
          ["5 to 13 Sep", "Bombardments and a deception operation at Kunsan, farther south."],
          ["15 Sep", "D-Day: the landing."]
        ]},
        "MacArthur's idea was simple and risky. Instead of trying to break through the perimeter head-on, he would land far behind the enemy front line, at a port near Seoul, and cut off the North Koreans' supply all at once. The place chosen was Incheon, which had every possible flaw."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Douglas MacArthur, 70 years old" },
        "The most famous general in the United States. His arguments were two: Incheon was so poorly situated that the enemy would not expect the attack, and a landing there would avoid a long winter of fighting. At the meeting on 23 August, he said that a landing at Kunsan, the safe option the Navy preferred, would not 'envelop' the enemy: it would only push him north.",
        { h: "The skeptics" },
        "General J. Lawton Collins, Army chief of staff, and Admiral Forrest Sherman, chief of naval operations, went to the meeting on 23 August, and many officers were skeptical. The Navy listed the problems: two narrow channels that could be mined, currents of 3 to 8 knots, enormous tides and high seawalls around the port. Commander Arlie Capps said the port had every natural and geographic disadvantage possible. The plan was approved on 28 August, after MacArthur won over Sherman by appealing to his affection for the Navy. Admiral James Doyle, who commanded the invasion force, is said to have been furious.",
        { h: "Edward Almond and X Corps" },
        "General Almond, MacArthur's chief of staff, was named to command X Corps, created only for the landing. He thought the operation would end the war quickly.",
        { h: "Eugene Clark, the tide spy" },
        "Navy lieutenant Eugene Clark and a team landed on 1 September on the island of Yonghung-do, off Incheon, and spent about two weeks collecting information on the beaches, the mud, the seawalls and the enemy's artillery positions. He found that the American tide tables were wrong and the Japanese ones were good, and that the North Koreans had not mined the channels properly. On the night of the landing, his team lit the lighthouse on Palmido island, at 12:50 a.m. on 15 September."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "On the United Nations side, sources speak of about 75,000 men and 261 ships (United States, South Korea, United Kingdom, Canada and other countries), or of about 40,000 infantry soldiers, depending on the count. The North Korean defense was some 6,500 men, with artillery on Wolmi-do island, in front of the port.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Incheon had every problem the Navy pointed out. The difference between high and low tide is, on average, 8.8 meters, and has been measured at 11 meters. At low tide, the bay turns into a sea of mud. There are no real beaches: there are high seawalls around the port. Ships had to enter through a narrow channel, the Flying Fish Channel, and everything depended on a few hours of high tide.",
        { h: "The landing in ten movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the deception. From 5 September, American aircraft bombed Kunsan, farther south, and on 6 September the Navy bombarded it too. On 11 September, B-29 bombers joined in and, on 12 and 13 September, a British ship, HMS Whitesand Bay, landed troops there. The goal was to make the North Koreans expect the attack at Kunsan.",
        "Second, the bombardment of Wolmi-do. On 10 September, 43 aircraft dropped 93 canisters of [[napalm|napalm]] on the island. On 13 September, destroyers entered the channel, at 7 in the morning, and fired almost a thousand rounds. The island's artillery answered and hit three ships: the Collett (seven times), the Gurke (three times) and the Lyman K. Swenson (twice). The destroyers found mines and blew them up with gunfire. On 14 September, the bombardment resumed.",
        "Third, on the morning of 15 September, at 6:30 a.m., the 3rd Battalion of the 5th Marine Regiment landed on Wolmi-do, at the so-called Green Beach, with nine M26 tanks. The island was taken at 11:15 a.m. and was secure by noon. On the United Nations side, there were 14 casualties. The North Koreans lost more than 200 dead and 136 prisoners.",
        "Fourth, the wait. After taking the island, the Marines had to wait for the tide to rise again, in the late afternoon. It was the price of choosing a place with tides like these: the first attack could be made in the morning, and the main one only in the afternoon.",
        "Fifth, the afternoon. At 5:24 p.m., eight landing craft with the Marines crossed the line of departure for Red Beach, north of the city. They had to climb the seawalls with ladders. Lieutenant Baldomero Lopez was killed covering a live grenade with his body, and later received the Medal of Honor. At 5:30 p.m., the first waves of the 1st Marine Regiment, under Lewis 'Chesty' Puller, reached Blue Beach, to the south, crossing 4 km of mud in 172 amphibious vehicles, supported by rockets fired from special boats.",
        "Sixth, the night. The Marine artillery was in position at 9:50 p.m. The city of Incheon was taken in less than 24 hours, with a few dozen Americans dead.",
        { h: "From Incheon to Seoul" },
        "Seventh, Kimpo airfield, to the east, was surrounded on the afternoon of 17 September and taken on the morning of the 18th. The North Koreans left some aircraft intact. By 22 September, 6,629 vehicles, 53,882 soldiers and about 23,000 tons of supplies had already landed. The 7th Division arrived by sea from the 18th.",
        "Eighth, the Eighth Army, commanded by Walton Walker, broke out of the Pusan Perimeter on 16 September. The North Koreans were in full retreat by the 22nd. Of the some 70,000 soldiers who surrounded the perimeter, 36,000 to 41,000 were killed or captured between 1 and 15 September, and some 30,000 escaped north.",
        "Ninth, Seoul. The Marines entered the city on 20 September. The fighting was slower than expected: eleven days to cover some 30 km. On the night of 25 September, Almond declared the city liberated, and MacArthur repeated the announcement the next day.",
        "Tenth, on 28 September, the last North Koreans were driven out or destroyed in Seoul. Within a month, the Americans had captured 135,000 North Korean prisoners."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The battle was cheap for the winners: 224 dead and 809 wounded on the United Nations side, against about 1,350 North Korean dead, according to one count. Seoul was retaken in 13 days.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The landing destroyed the North Korean position in the south, and on 30 September the United Nations forces began to advance into North Korea. A few weeks later, China entered the war and pushed the UN forces back. The war ended in 1953, with the border more or less in the same place as before.",
        "The historian Spencer Tucker said Incheon was the only large-scale United States operation that succeeded without ambiguity in the following 40 years. It is still a model of amphibious landing today.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "One critic, the historian Russell Stolfi, says the slowness of the 11 days to Seoul took away much of the landing's effect. Incheon itself was taken in 24 hours." },
        { note: "The leak: the head of a North Korean and Japanese spy ring, arrested in early September, had a copy of the plan." },
        { note: "The Marines were not invited to the meeting at which MacArthur presented the plan, although they were the ones who would lead the landing, and this caused friction." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  dienbienphu: {
    war: "Indochina War (1946–1954): the siege of Dien Bien Phu, 13 March to 7 May 1954",
    hook: "The French chose the valley; the Vietnamese general chose the hills around it and dragged the guns up there.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Since 1946, France had been fighting in Vietnam against the [[Viet Minh|viet minh]], who wanted independence. By 1953, the war was dragging on, and the Vietnamese had invaded parts of Laos. The new French commander, General Henri Navarre, took over in May 1953 with a single order: to create conditions for an honorable political exit.",
        "Navarre chose to fight. The idea came from another officer, Colonel Louis Berteil: to build a fortified base, supplied only by air, that would block the Viet Minh's paths to Laos and force them to attack it. The model was the battle of Nà Sản, in 1952, where the French held out in a base like this. The place chosen, in the end, was the valley of Dien Bien Phu, in northwestern Vietnam.",
        { h: "The road to the valley" },
        { timeline: [
          ["May 1953", "Navarre takes over the French command."],
          ["20 Nov", "Operation Castor begins: about 9,000 soldiers are dropped by parachute and by plane in three days."],
          ["9 Dec", "A column of 2,100 men leaves Lai Châu to reinforce the base. Only 185 arrive."],
          ["25–26 Jan 1954", "The quick attack planned by Giáp is canceled."],
          ["13 Mar", "The battle begins."],
          ["7 May", "The base surrenders."]
        ]},
        "There was a decisive difference from Nà Sản. At Dien Bien Phu, the Vietnamese held the surrounding hills, were about four times as numerous and had far more artillery than the French imagined. And the French supply base, in Hanoi, was some 250 km away."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Võ Nguyên Giáp, 42 years old" },
        "He was a History teacher before the war, and became the Viet Minh's general. He planned the attack as a 'quick strike, quick victory' operation, set for 25 January. But the plan was discovered by the French, and Giáp canceled it and changed the strategy to 'steady fighting, steady advance', a slow siege. Giáp called this the hardest decision of his life.",
        { h: "Christian de Castries, base commander" },
        "Commander of the garrison, he organized, from December, the strongpoints around the center. As the days went by, leadership in practice passed to Colonel Pierre Langlais, from 24 March, according to accounts, although the date is disputed.",
        { h: "Charles Piroth, the man of the guns" },
        "Chief of the French artillery, he had only one arm. He assured his colleagues that the Viet Minh would never manage to bring heavy guns to the hills. When the guns opened fire, on 13 March, he saw he was wrong. He killed himself around 15 March.",
        { h: "Marcel Bigeard and Geneviève de Galard" },
        "Marcel Bigeard, a paratrooper commander, was one of the officers who resisted most. Geneviève de Galard was one of the 15 women on the evacuation flights. She was trapped at the base when her plane was destroyed, and worked in the field hospital until the surrender. Historians disagree about the nickname 'Angel of Dien Bien Phu'."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "On 13 March, the French garrison was some 10,800 men, of whom about 9,000 were combat troops, and rose to some 14,000 by 7 May (close to 16,000, counting reinforcements dropped by parachute). They were paratroopers, the [[Foreign Legion|legião estrangeira]], Algerian and Moroccan [[tirailleurs|tirailleurs]], and Vietnamese, Laotian and Cambodian soldiers. They had 10 light M24 tanks and six batteries of 105 mm guns, plus one battery of four 155 mm howitzers.",
        "The Viet Minh gathered some 49,500 fighters and 15,000 support men, in five divisions: the 304th, 308th, 312th, 316th and the 351st, which was heavy artillery only. The guns and anti-aircraft weapons, mostly of Soviet origin, and some American 105 mm howitzers, all coming through China, were around four for every one of the French. Sources speak of 144 to 200 heavy pieces.",
        { h: "The logistics" },
        "The Viet Minh carried all this along trails in the forest and the mountains, at night, hidden from the aircraft. The number of people is debated: sources speak of 100,000 to 200,000 porters, and of 20,000 to 60,000 bicycles, pushed, not pedaled, with about 200 kg of cargo each, according to accounts. There were also about 600 Soviet trucks, according to one source. The guns were carried disassembled, largely by hand, up the slopes.",
        { note: "The logistics numbers are the ones that vary most between sources, partly because the story became a propaganda legend. That it was a feat of logistics, everyone agrees." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "A valley surrounded by hills and forest, with the rivers and the airstrip in the middle. The French occupied the valley floor, in strongpoints with women's names: Gabrielle to the north, Béatrice to the northeast, Anne-Marie to the northwest, Huguette to the west, Claudine to the south, Dominique northeast of the center, Eliane, which became a key position near the center, and Isabelle, some 6 km to the south, to protect the reserve airstrip. The Vietnamese took the hills around.",
        { h: "The siege in ten movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the position. The French expected the Viet Minh to attack and smash themselves against the strongpoints. The Vietnamese dug tunnels and shelters into the slopes, and placed the guns on the back slopes of the hills, from which they came out to fire and returned to hide. They fired by [[direct fire|fogo direto]], from camouflaged shelters, and the French could not carry out [[counter-battery fire|contrabateria]].",
        "Second, Béatrice. On 13 March, the guns opened fire, and the strongpoint, to the northeast, fell after 1 a.m. on 14 March. The two commanders of the site, Major Paul Pégot and Lieutenant Colonel Jules Gaucher, died right at the start.",
        "Third, Gabrielle. The airstrip was closed on 14 March, and supplies began to fall only by parachute. Gabrielle, to the north, fell at 8 a.m. on 15 March, after a French counterattack that failed.",
        "Fourth, on 17 March, the Tai troops defending Anne-Marie, to the northwest, deserted or vanished into the fog, and the French abandoned the post. A lull followed until 30 March. On 28 March, the French destroyed 17 Viet Minh anti-aircraft machine guns.",
        "Fifth, Dominique and Eliane. From 30 March to 5 April, the Vietnamese attacked in mass. Dominique 1 and 2 fell on 30 March. Bigeard refused to abandon Eliane 4. The attacks cost the Vietnamese dearly, and, after 5 April, Giáp turned to the [[sapa|sapa]]: trenches ever closer to the French positions.",
        "Sixth, April. The French retook Eliane 1 on 10 April. On 18 April, they abandoned Huguette 6, and Huguette 1 fell on 22 April. With that, the Viet Minh came to control more than 90% of the airstrip. Isabelle, isolated since 30 March, was running out of water and ammunition.",
        "Seventh, 1 May. The Vietnamese took Eliane 1, Dominique 3 and Huguette 5.",
        "Eighth, 6 May. The Viet Minh used [[katyusha|katyusha]] launchers for the first time, and exploded a mine dug beneath Eliane 2.",
        "Ninth, 7 May. The general attack began. Around 5 p.m., De Castries told General Cogny, in Hanoi, that the situation was grave. The last strongpoint surrendered after 6:20 p.m.",
        "Tenth, Isabelle, in the south. It remained isolated until the end. About 70 men escaped to Laos."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "On the French side, there were 1,142 killed in combat (up to 2,293, by other historians' count), 429 who died of wounds, 1,606 missing and 11,721 prisoners, of whom 4,436 were wounded. They also lost 62 aircraft and 10 tanks. On the Vietnamese side, the counts range from 8,000 dead and 15,000 wounded (Western estimate) to 23,000 to 25,000 casualties (Asian historians). The official Vietnamese numbers speak of 13,930 casualties (4,020 dead).",
        "Of the prisoners, the Red Cross evacuated 858 of the most seriously wounded at the end of May. Only 3,290 were returned, four months later, and the death rate in captivity is calculated at about 60%, although this number is contested. Some 200 French managed to escape.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The Geneva Conference began on 8 May, a day after the surrender. In July, the agreement divided Vietnam at the 17th parallel. The French government of Joseph Laniel fell in June, and Pierre Mendès France took over. It was the end of French rule in Indochina. In Algeria, the National Liberation Front saw in the result proof that a colonial army could be defeated.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The United States paid about 80% of French spending in Indochina, and gave aircraft, weapons and mechanics. Thirty-nine American pilots of a CIA-linked company (CAT) flew 682 supply missions, and two of them, James McGovern and Wallace Buford, died. Sources give different numbers of pilots (24 or 39). An American bombing plan, Operation Vulture, was never carried out: Admiral Radford thought it was already too late." },
        { note: "The duration: the count of '56 days' holds from the first attack, 13 March. The French garrison, however, stayed in the valley 167 days, since Operation Castor, in November." },
        { note: "The supply: Dien Bien Phu received about 7,000 tons by air in the 56 days of the siege, an average of some 120 tons a day, against the 200 the base needed, and it lay some 250 km from Hanoi. Years later, at Khe Sanh (1968), the Americans, some 60 to 70 km from Dong Ha, received about 17,000 tons in 77 or 78 days, depending on the count." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  baiadosporcos: {
    war: "Cold War: the Bay of Pigs invasion, in Cuba (17 to 19 April 1961)",
    hook: "About 1,400 Cuban exiles landed to overthrow Fidel Castro and, within three days, were dead, captured or cornered on a beach.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1952, Fulgencio Batista seized power in Cuba, and several groups took up arms against him. One of them was the 26th of July Movement, of Fidel Castro, which fought a [[guerrilla war|guerrilha]] in the Sierra Maestra. Batista resigned and fled on 31 December 1958.",
        "The new government nationalized American companies and drew closer to the Soviet Union. The United States reacted with an [[embargo|embargo]] (in October 1960) and cut relations. On 17 March 1960, the [[CIA|cia]] presented to the National Security Council a plan to replace Castro's government with another, with an approved budget of 13 million dollars. Documents from the Eisenhower presidential library show that he had neither ordered nor approved a landing.",
        { h: "The road to the beach" },
        { timeline: [
          ["17 Mar 1960", "The CIA presents the plan to the National Security Council. In April the recruitment of the exiles begins."],
          ["Oct 1960", "The United States imposes the embargo on Cuba."],
          ["18 Nov", "Allen Dulles and Richard Bissell, of the CIA, explain the plan to President-elect John Kennedy."],
          ["28 Jan 1961", "Kennedy is briefed on the plan for a landing at Trinidad, which would end up rejected."],
          ["4 Apr", "Kennedy approves the Bay of Pigs plan, with a longer airstrip and less visible US involvement."],
          ["15 Apr", "B-26 aircraft bomb Cuban airfields."],
          ["17 Apr", "The exiles land."]
        ]},
        "The exiles formed [[Brigade 2506|brigada 2506]], the armed arm of the Cuban Revolutionary Democratic Front, financed by the CIA and trained in Guatemala. The region of the bay, in southern Cuba, is surrounded by a great swamp, with few paths."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "John F. Kennedy, 43 years old" },
        "President for less than three months. He inherited a plan that had been made by his predecessors and the CIA, and wanted to give it less American visibility. After the first air strikes caused a scandal at the United Nations, he canceled the following strikes, on 16 April. This left part of Castro's air force intact, which would prove decisive.",
        { h: "Fidel Castro, 34 years old" },
        "He was woken in the early hours, around 3:15 a.m. on 17 April, put the militia on alert, ordered the air force to attack and went to the battle front. He reached the sugar mill closest to the bay at 4 a.m. on 18 April. Initial command of the counterattack went to José Ramón Fernández, but Castro later took over personally.",
        { h: "The exiles" },
        "José Pérez San Román commanded the brigade, and Erneido Oliva was second in command. Both lost their radios right at the start, when the sets got wet. The B-26 pilots were partly exiles, partly Americans hired by the CIA and volunteers from the Alabama Air National Guard.",
        { h: "The planners" },
        "Allen Dulles, director of the CIA, and Richard Bissell, the man responsible for the plan, were forced to leave office in early 1962, together with deputy director Charles Cabell."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The brigade had more than 1,400 men in five infantry battalions and one of [[paratroopers|paraquedistas]], five light M41 tanks, 16 B-26 bombers, five cargo ships and four troop transports. On the other side were about 25,000 soldiers of the Cuban army, some 200,000 militiamen, 9,000 policemen, Soviet-made T-34-85 tanks and SU-100 self-propelled guns. After the attack of 15 April, Castro's Air Force still had at least four T-33 jets, four propeller-driven Sea Fury fighters and five or six B-26s.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The region is a low coast, of swamp and coral reefs, with the Bay of Pigs (Bahía de Cochinos) cutting into the land. There were two main beaches: Playa Girón, to the south, which the invaders called Blue Beach, and Playa Larga, some 35 km to the northwest, Red Beach. Few roads crossed the swamp.",
        { h: "The invasion in three days" },
        "On 15 April, eight B-26s attacked three airfields near Havana and one at Santiago, and returned. The American government denied any involvement: Adlai Stevenson, ambassador to the UN, denied it, and it later became known that the CIA had lied to him. Part of the Cuban air force survived.",
        "On the night of 16 to 17 April, a false operation near Bahía Honda diverted Castro's attention. At midnight, the ships entered the bay. Around 1 a.m., divers and soldiers landed at Playa Girón. At Playa Larga, the landing was lit up by searchlights, and the site was changed in a hurry. The coral reefs and engine failures delayed the landing. A scuffle with a jeep of militiamen raised the alarm.",
        "From 6:30 a.m., Castro's aircraft attacked the ships. The Houston was hit at 6:50 a.m. and beached two hours later, with only some 270 soldiers ashore. At 9:30 a.m., the Río Escondido, which carried fuel, ammunition, food, medicine and the radio that linked the brigade to the aircraft, was sunk by rockets. Around 7:30 a.m., about 177 paratroopers were dropped. Some 30 landed far from the target and lost their equipment in the swamps, but other groups held roadblocks for two days.",
        "During the night of 17 to 18, the troops at Red Beach suffered successive counterattacks with ammunition running out. At 1 a.m., about 2,100 Cubans attacked some 370 men of the brigade. Oliva ordered a withdrawal by 5 a.m., and Playa Larga fell around 10:30 a.m. on the 18th. Oliva proposed fleeing to the Escambray mountains and fighting as guerrillas, but San Román decided to hold the beachhead.",
        "On 18 April, at 2 p.m., Kennedy received a telegram from the Soviet premier, Nikita Khrushchev, warning the United States not to enter Cuba. That afternoon, B-26s flown by hired Americans and by exile crews attacked a column of buses and armored trucks.",
        "On 19 April, the last air mission, called Mad Dog Flight, had five B-26s, four of them flown by Americans. Two were shot down, and four Americans died. United States Navy jets, unmarked, flew patrol, but did not enter combat. At 10 a.m. a tank battle began. At 2 p.m., Oliva ordered a withdrawal to Girón, and the brigade ran out of mortar and bazooka ammunition. At night, the destroyers USS Eaton and Murray tried to pick up survivors, but pulled back under fire from Cuban tanks."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The brigade surrendered on 19 or 20 April, depending on the source. There were about 1,200 prisoners (1,202, according to Wikipedia). Between 67 and 114 men of the brigade died in combat, depending on the count, and others were executed. Four Americans died. On the Cuban side, 176 dead in combat and about 2,000 militiamen dead or wounded, a number that the source itself calls vague.",
        "In December 1962, Castro and the lawyer James Donovan agreed to the exchange of 1,113 prisoners for 53 million dollars in food and medicine. The men returned to Miami at the end of that month, and President Kennedy received the brigade on 29 December.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The failure made Castro a national hero and pushed Cuba closer to the Soviet Union. In 1962, the missile crisis came. On 21 April 1961, Kennedy said in a press conference that victory has a hundred fathers, and defeat is an orphan, and that he was the one responsible. On 22 April, he created a commission under General Maxwell Taylor, which, in June, pointed to, among the causes, the lack of aircraft, the limits on weaponry and air strikes, the loss of important ships and the lack of ammunition.",
        "After the invasion, Kennedy had the Pentagon draw up covert operations against Castro, Operation Mongoose. Dulles, Cabell and Bissell lost their posts.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "How much air support there actually was: Kennedy canceled the second strike, on 16 April, but on 18 and 19 April he authorized a limited B-26 mission with American pilots and the patrol of unmarked jets. So it is imprecise to say there was no air support at all after the cancellation." },
        { note: "The dates: English Wikipedia speaks of surrender on 20 April. In Cuba, the victory at Girón is celebrated on 19 April." },
        { note: "The prisoners: on 8 September 1961, 14 of them were convicted of crimes predating the invasion, and five were executed. In March and April 1962, 1,179 were tried for treason and sentenced to 30 years. Nine died of suffocation in a truck, during transport to Havana." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

